course: dictionary
lesson: 3118
title: Words about voting
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show you which one fits. «New Zealand uses MMP: each voter has an electorate vote for a local MP and a party vote.» The words vote and MP are clues that this is about elections, and an electorate here is the area where voters choose one local MP.
explain: Try the simplest meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense. An electorate can mean all the people allowed to vote, or it can mean an area that elects one MP. Read on to decide which one the writer means.
explain: «The politician tried to appeal to the electorate.» Appeal to the electorate means try to win the voters over. Here electorate names a group of people. Whenever you meet the word, ask yourself: is this the people, or is it the place?
fact: An electorate is all the people in a country or area who are allowed to vote in an election.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, an electorate is an area that elects one member of parliament, called a constituency in the UK.
fact: «A divided electorate» means voters who disagree with each other.
fact: «New Zealand's Māori electorates, set up in 1867, are seats for voters on the Māori roll.»
fact: The Treaty of Waitangi of 1840 is a founding agreement between Māori chiefs and the British Crown.
exercise: «The politician wanted to appeal to the electorate.» Who is the electorate here? | *The voters | The hall where the speech is given | The politician's family | Shopkeepers in the town
exercise: In New Zealand, «my electorate» means what? | *My voting district, the area that elects one local MP | My school's sports team | All the money the country spends | My favourite park
exercise: «New Zealand's Māori electorates, set up in 1867, are seats for voters on the Māori roll.» In this sentence, what does set up mean? | Painted | Sold | Broken | *Started or created
test: Which meaning fits «the whole electorate was asked to vote»? | *All the people who are allowed to vote | A sports team | A type of bridge | Trees and plants in a country
test: Why might a politician care about the young members of the electorate? | Because young people always live far away | *Because they are voters the politician wants to win over | Because young people cannot read | Because they own the parliament
exam: Which sentence shows an electorate as an area rather than a group of people? | Politicians tried to appeal to the electorate | The electorate was asked to vote in the election | *In New Zealand, my electorate elects one local MP | A divided electorate argued all night
